    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d55">

                <pb facs="0066" n="54" sameAs="frus:frus1943v04/pg54"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.42790H/64: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Afghanistan</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kabul</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-03-17T10:00:00+04:00">March 17, 1943—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 18—10:13 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>58. There is a possibility that if we could immediately furnish a first-class
                    irrigation engineer and four surveyors we might be able to induce the Afghan
                    Government to get rid of the Japanese now in the service of the Ministry of
                    Public Works and referred to in paragraph 7 of my telegram 74, September 15,
                        1942.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d55-f1"/> These Japanese are now again employed
                    in the Kandahar Province. See also telegram 44, February 21, 1943.<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d55-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Please telegraph whether such American engineers could be supplied.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d55-f1" n="68" xml:id="d55-f1-content">
